  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q23-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "23",
    "prompt": "Solve the equation\n$$2\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^2+\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)-15=0.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=2,\\frac12,\\frac{-3+\\sqrt5}2,\\frac{-3-\\sqrt5}2$.\n\nSet $u=x+\\frac1x$, giving $u=\\frac52$ or $u=-3$.",
      "worked": "Let $u=x+\\frac1x$, where $x\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2u^2+u-15&=0\\\\u&=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{1-4(2)(-15)}}4\\\\u&=\\frac{-1\\pm11}4.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $u=\\frac52$ or $u=-3$.\nFor $u=\\frac52$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x+\\frac1x&=\\frac52\\\\2x^2-5x+2&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{5\\pm\\sqrt{25-16}}4\\\\x&=\\frac{5\\pm3}4.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $x=2$ or $x=\\frac12$.\nFor $u=-3$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x+\\frac1x&=-3\\\\x^2+3x+1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{-3\\pm\\sqrt{9-4}}2\\\\x&=\\frac{-3\\pm\\sqrt5}2.\\end{align*}$$\nAll four values are nonzero. Therefore $x=2,\\frac12,\\frac{-3+\\sqrt5}2,\\frac{-3-\\sqrt5}2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 152
  },
